\section{Introduction}

Modern planetary systems exhibit tightly coupled dynamics across geopolitical,
ecological, energetic, informational, and financial domains. Traditional
centralized stabilization mechanisms fail to address these interactions due to
structural fragility, limited observability, strategic regional behavior, and
the absence of a neutral global coordination substrate. Project
\textit{Cosmos-Harmony}, designed by C.~Matthew~Blackwell, introduces a
decentralized, cryptographically verified, closed-loop autonomous intelligence
runtime capable of harmonizing global stress trajectories under sovereignty
constraints and stochastic astrophysical forcing.

Cosmos-Harmony integrates five foundational pillars into a unified operational
architecture:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Astrophysical Entropy Ingestion.}
    High-entropy cosmic telemetry from pulsar timing arrays, CMB anisotropies,
    gravitational-wave backgrounds, and solar flux variance provides an
    unforgeable stochastic baseline for unbiased global coordination.

    \item \textbf{Post-Quantum Cryptographic Seeding.}
    Regional private types $\theta_i$ are committed using lattice-based
    cryptography and verified through zero-knowledge proofs, ensuring that
    regional constraints and sovereignty preferences are provably satisfied
    without revealing sensitive internal state.

    \item \textbf{Distributed Model Predictive Control (MPC).}
    Autonomous regional nodes solve local MPC subproblems over finite horizons,
    incorporating Ex-Post Incentive Compatibility (EPIC) constraints and
    type-dependent feasibility sets $\mathcal{U}_i(\theta_i)$ to guarantee
    strategic alignment and prevent destabilizing deviations.

    \item \textbf{Global Harmonization and Actuator Deployment.}
    Verified control vectors $u_t^*$ are translated into physical interventions
    across energy grids, food logistics networks, financial buffers, and
    information channels. Sovereignty-preserving actuator caps enforce strict
    compliance with regional feasibility boundaries.

    \item \textbf{Decentralized Satellite Mesh Consensus.}
    A distributed orbital mesh verifies zero-knowledge proofs, forms
    cryptographic consensus, synchronizes epoch clocks, and broadcasts the
    updated global state, closing the loop and feeding verified data into the
    next optimization horizon.
\end{enumerate}

The Cosmos-Harmony runtime is mathematically grounded in a distributed Lyapunov
stability framework. The global state $x_t$ evolves according to decentralized
regional controls $u_{i,t}$ and bounded astrophysical noise $w_t$, with
stability guaranteed by the contraction condition
\[
V(x_{t+1}) - V(x_t) \le -\alpha V(x_t) + \beta\|w_t\|^2,
\]
for $\alpha > 0$. Monte Carlo simulations across diverse regional configurations
empirically validate this contraction, demonstrating that decentralized vetoes,
regional autonomy, and cryptographic verification do not impede global
harmonization.

Cosmos-Harmony constitutes a complete autonomous intelligence architecture:
neutral in its stochastic foundation, secure in its cryptographic guarantees,
optimal in its distributed control logic, and globally coherent through its
orbital consensus substrate. This manuscript presents the full theoretical
framework, cryptographic protocols, decentralized optimization routines, and
empirical validation results that define the Cosmos-Harmony Codex.